Subject: SUMMARY: The OSF/1 3.2 FAQ revisited...
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075320706370


Hi!

Some days ago I asked to following question to the list:

> I just upgraded to OSF/1 3.2 and ran into the problem anybody here seems to
> have:
> 
> Too many users logged on already.
> Try again later.
> rlogin: connection closed.
>
> What is my problem? Do I have a wrong OSF-USR license?
> # lmf list
> Product                   Status                     Users: Total
> Active
> 
> OSF-DEV                   active                            unlimited
> OSF-SVR                   active                            unlimited
> OSF-USR                   active                            2          2    
> OSF-USR                   active, multiple                  2          2    
[ del del del ]

This was truly a frequently answered question. :-) I got 22 answers, most of
them pointing me directly to the correct solution and the other pointing to
other probable mistakes.

Selden E Ball Jr wrote, as many others did:
> OSF/1 V3.x ships with a 2 user license automatically installed.
> This overrides any unlimited-user license.
> You have to delete the 2 user license in order for the 0 unit
> unlimited license to be effective: 0 + 2 = 2 
> 
> OSF/1 v3.x also requires that you install the OSF-BASE license PAK
> that came with the system. V2.0 didn't enforce that requirement.
>
> Check to see what licenses are actually loaded.
> lmf list
> If that is the problem, then you must "lmf delete" the 2 user license
> and then do "lmf reset"

This worked fine for me. I had to use the long syntax for the lmf delete,
since both licenses are OSF-USR licenses.

Some other problems you may discover with OSF/1 3.x:
- the OSF-BASE license must be loaded for the system to work
- the files update and update0 must be present in the root directory
  for the system to work for reasons I do not know.

